# PHASE 9 — PREMIUM WEBSITE RELOAD / INITIAL LOADING ANIMATION

Inspect the existing website's initial loading and reload experience.

This is a visual enhancement, not a replacement for actual application initialization.

## 9.1 First inspect existing implementation

Determine:

- Whether a reload animation already exists.
- Whether a global loading screen exists.
- Whether route transitions already have loading states.
- Whether existing animations are reusable.
- Whether loading state is correctly connected to application readiness.

### Required decision

If no suitable loading animation exists, implement a premium initial loading experience.

If one already exists, do not create a duplicate system.

Improve the existing implementation only when a real quality problem is identified.

If the existing animation is already premium and reliable, preserve it.

## 9.2 Premium visual concept

Create a premium loading experience that matches the existing homepage brand identity.

The design should include:

- Elegant full-screen backdrop.
- A clean, premium visual surface.
- Brand logo or approved existing logo asset.
- A circular progress indicator.
- Smooth circular progress animation.
- Subtle glow or gradient treatment.
- Refined typography.
- A short brand-aligned loading message.
- Smooth entrance and exit transitions.

The logo should remain visually prominent.

The circular indicator should animate smoothly around or near the logo.

Use the actual brand logo from the project.

Do not invent a new logo or replace the brand identity.

Do not use random external images.

## 9.3 Genuine loading state

The animation must reflect actual application readiness as far as technically possible.

Do not fake a 100% progress completion while the application is still unavailable.

If actual percentage progress cannot be measured, use an honest indeterminate circular animation rather than pretending to report exact progress.

The loading overlay should:

1. Appear when the application genuinely needs initial loading.
2. Remain visible while critical initialization is in progress.
3. Disappear when the application is ready or when a recoverable error state is presented.
4. Avoid unnecessary delays.
5. Never block the application permanently.

## 9.4 Technical and accessibility requirements

- Do not intercept or break browser refresh behavior.
- Do not modify browser history incorrectly.
- Do not create an infinite loading loop.
- Do not show a full-screen loader on every minor state update.
- Do not show unnecessary loading animations during normal component re-renders.
- Support reduced-motion preferences.
- Preserve keyboard and screen-reader accessibility.
- Avoid layout shifts.
- Keep animation lightweight.
- Ensure the overlay works on desktop and mobile.

If an application initialization error occurs, show an appropriate recovery or retry experience instead of hiding the error behind an endless animation.

